## 1. The Big Idea

_Your body fat picks the phase; lock your maintenance calories first so the gauge is calibrated; then hold a tight 10–20% oscillation instead of bulking._ The poster is a calibrated control instrument a lifter pins up and re-reads every few weeks — not a motivational image.

## 2. Visual Metaphor & Structure

**A calibrated control instrument with two coupled parts**, in the same engineering-diagnostic spirit as Part 1.1's fault matrix — but composed differently so the pillar stays varied while staying coherent.

- **Hero — the Phase Gauge:** a single **vertical calibrated body-fat scale** (a tank-level / instrument gauge, _not_ a diet-app progress bar), leanest at the top, highest body fat at the bottom. Three **operating bands** are marked on it — Lean Bulk, Decision, Recomp — each annotated with its calorie delta. A **"YOU ARE HERE" marker** lets the reader plot their DEXA/InBody number from Part 1.1. A **looping oscillation arrow** spans the ~10–20% region (the band most experienced naturals live in). The metaphor is exact: the decision tree _is_ a position on one axis, so the article's branching logic becomes a single reading off a gauge.
- **Prerequisite — the Calibration readout (Step 0):** a compact **trend-monitor chartlet** showing the 2-week lock-in — a jagged daily-weigh-in line overlaid with a smooth 7-day-average line — plus a small result table (flat / dropped / climbed → maintenance verdict). This is the "zero the instrument before you trust the needle" step, and it visually teaches "true weight = the smooth line, not the jagged one."

Why this metaphor fits: the article's own engine is _measurement and calibration_ ("the only way to know your real TDEE is to measure it"), and its decision logic is _one-dimensional_ (body fat %). A gauge you must calibrate before reading is the literal geometry of both ideas at once.

## 3. Layout — exact composition

Orientation: **portrait / tall** (consistent with Part 1.1; reads top-to-bottom inline in the blog and saves cleanly to a phone).

Zones, top to bottom:

1. **Kicker (top-left):** mono tag `FIT · PART 1.2 — RECOMP, CUT OR BULK`, preceded by a short horizontal tick/rule so it reads as an instrument label.
2. **Headline block (upper, large):** headline in the display face (copy in §4), allow two lines; one-line subhead beneath in muted secondary text.
3. **Step 0 — Calibration readout (compact band, ~18–22% of height):** label it clearly as the _prerequisite_ — `STEP 0 · ZERO THE INSTRUMENT`. Inside:
   - A small **two-week trend chartlet**: x-axis = Day 1→14 with a faint Wk1 | Wk2 divider; a **jagged daily line** (low contrast, captioned `daily weigh-ins — noise`) and a **smooth 7-day-average line** (higher contrast, captioned `7-day average — TRUE WEIGHT, the signal`). Mark `Wk 1 avg` and `Wk 2 avg` as two dots being compared.
   - A **3-row result table** to the right of (or beneath) the chartlet (copy in §4).
   - A one-line tie-back showing the three deltas hanging off the zero datum: `RECOMP ±0 · CUT −500 · LEAN BULK +200–300` — these align/connect visually upward into the matching gauge bands.
4. **Step 1 — the Phase Gauge (hero, give it the most area, ~45–50% of height):**
   - A clean **vertical calibrated scale**, `BODY FAT %`, leanest at top. Tick marks at meaningful values (e.g. ~8, 12, 18, 20, 24%). Small body-composition tick-icon at the axis head.
   - **Three operating bands**, each a horizontal zone on the gauge with: a mono **phase tag**, the **body-fat range**, the **calorie delta** (loud), and one short action line. Top→bottom:
     - `LEAN BULK` · `< 12% · abs visible` · **+200–300 kcal**
     - `DECISION` · `12–17%` · **your call**
     - `RECOMP — default` · `18%+` · **±0 (maintenance)**
   - **Oscillation loop:** a looping arrow drawn _over_ the gauge spanning ~10–20%, labelled `THE BAND` (copy in §4) — lean bulk up, mini-cut down, repeat.
   - **"YOU ARE HERE" marker:** a needle/caret on the scale with `▶ YOU` and a prompt to plot the Part 1.1 number.
   - **Struck-through redline (the trap):** at the high-body-fat end (below the Recomp band, where a dirty bulk lands you), a clearly **rejected** zone — `✕ DIRTY BULK` with the net-2 kg math (copy in §4). This is the only place the _danger/alarm_ semantic color is used; the three phases are NOT danger-colored (see §5).
5. **Footer:** quiet series mark `Fit — Aesthetic & Strength`, plus a tiny technical annotation `TDEE = BMR + TEF + EAT + NEAT · re-anchor every 6–8 weeks`.

**Eye path:** kicker → headline → Step 0 (calibrate: jagged vs smooth, find your zero) → drop into the Phase Gauge → plot YOU → read your band + delta → notice the oscillation loop and the struck-through bulk → out. The numbered Step 0 / Step 1 framing keeps the logical order (calibrate _then_ steer) even though the gauge is the visual hero.

